# wforth

a Forth that passes core tests
- uses indirect threaded code (ITC)
- easy to port

## usage

to build, use 'make' with a profile of your choice:

    make wforth             # x86_64
    make wforth-maxtext     # x86_64
    make wforth-rv64        # RISC-V 64
    make wforth-aarch64     # AArch64

the 'maxtext' profile implements most of its words as native words in assembly. other profiles instead only natively assemble a minimal number of words.

building some profiles might require a cross-compiler (the Makefile assumes x86_64 as host). testing some profiles might require 'qemu-user' to be available for the target architecture.

to use:

    ./wforth
    ./wforth-maxtext
    ./wforth-rv64
    ./wforth-aarch64

to test:

    make test-wforth
    make test-wforth-maxtext
    make test-wforth-rv64
    make test-wforth-aarch64

## porting

a wforth system consists of the following words:

    ! # #> ' ( * */ + +! +loop , - . ." .( / : ; < <# = > @ [
    ['] \ ] 0< 0= 1+ 1- 2! 2* 2/ 2@ 2drop 2dup 2over 2swap abs
    accept again ahead align aligned allot and base begin bl
    >body bye c! c, c@ catch cell+ cells [char] char+ char
    chars cmove cmove> compare compile, constant count cr
    create decimal depth do does> drop d>s ?dup dup else emit
    evaluate execute exit false fill find fm/mod here hex hold
    i if immediate >in invert j leave literal loop lshift m* m+
    max min */mod /mod mod move name>string name>word negate
    nip >number or over parse-name postpone quit >r r> r@
    recurse refill repeat rot rp! rp@ rshift #s s" s>d
    search-wordlist sign sm/rem source sp! sp@ space spaces
    state /string swap then throw true type u. u< um*
    um/mod unloop until variable while within word words xor 

    compare-nocase ?immediate interpret interpret-step ?upper
    ?variable WFORTH um*/mod

    (abort-msg) (br) (create) (current) (does) (doing) (doing0)
    (doing1) (doloop) (finish) (handler) (here) (jmp) (leave)
    (+loop) (loop) (parse-name) (po) (push) (quit) (refs)
    (sign) (str) (word)

the following minimal set must be implemented natively:

    and bye cells c@ c! depth drop dup emit = exit false @ > <
    lshift max - min m+ m* or + refill r@ r> rot rp@ rp! rshift
    sm/rem sp@ sp! */mod * ! swap >r true 2/ 2swap u< um/mod
    um* xor

    : (here) ( -- a-addr ) push address of '.hereptr'
    : (quit) ( -- ) see fragments/unimplemented/sys_quit.wf.S
    : (refs) ( -- ) push address of '.refdata'
    : um*/mod ( ud u u -- ud u ) m*/, unsigned, with remainder

    um*/mod is used by the interpreter, >NUMBER and '#', but
    only in two cases:

    ud 1 u um*/mod
    ud u 1 um*/mod

- all words are listed in 'lists/core.txt', mapped to a file name.
- all words are defined somewhere in 'fragments/', one per file, with a header line specifying name and immediate status.
- a "profile" is a folder path in 'fragments/'.
- the bootstrap compiler "build.sh" finds one '.wf.fr', '.wf.S' or '.wf.c' for each word, starting from the profile folder and moving up to 'fragments/'.
- all '.wf.fr' and '.wf.S' are copied into a '.waf.S' assembly file alongside generated word definitions and directives.
- the bootstrap assembler 'wforth.awk' produces the final assembly file from '.waf.S'
- the assembly file is compiled together with needed '.wf.c' files.

to port wforth to a new platform:

1. create a profile folder, like 'fragments/myarch16'.
2. copy 'fragments/unimplemented/*' to your profile.
3. check out one of the unimplemented definitions, like 'xor.wf.S':

    // native word .cxor xor
    .cxor:
    mv a0, fp // a0: first argument, fp: ITC instruction pointer
    call unimplemented

4. use text processing tools like 'sed' to convert these calls to the call signature and assembly dialect of your target architecture.
5. edit the following files (in 'unimplemented/', these are based on RV64 and annotated):

    wforth.S
    sys_br.wf.S
    sys_jmp.wf.S
    sys_push.wf.S
    sys_doing.wf.S
    sys_doing0.wf.S
    sys_doing1.wf.S
    sys_variable.wf.S
    sys_docol.wf.S
    codegen-c.sh

6. build your profile

    CC=myarch16-linux-gnu-gcc ./build.sh wforth-myarch16 myarch16 core dbg

7. if compiled successfully, run the test suite:

    cat test/src/all.fr | qemu-myarch16 -L /usr/myarch16-linux-gnu ./wforth-myarch16

8. if wforth terminates with 'UNIMPLEMENTED !' you're on the right track.
9. write a proper implementation in 'store.wf.S'.
10. compile again.
11. test again.
12. go back to step 8 until all words are implemented and the tests all pass.

